Give the wait for a resource an end

The photograph of run 548 says where the boot stopped, and it says it
twice: the mark that says a personality is being started is there, and
the mark that says it came back never came.  The two photographs taken
minutes apart are the same, with only the beacon further along, so the
boot is not slow -- it is stopped.

It is stopped in startCandidate(), which is where a personality's start
begins:

    if ((this != gIOResources) && (this != gIOUserResources)) {
        // stall for any nub resources
        checkResources();
        // stall for any driver resources
        service->checkResources();
    }

and checkResources() ends in checkResource(), which ends in

    waitForService( table );

with no timeout.  The resource the platform expert waits for is the BSD
resource, and this port's storage driver matches for observation only
and never publishes it -- so the wait had no end.  The userland does not
need the BSD resource: the root volume the kernel boots from is the
mockfs image in RAM, and mockfs is the file system that mounts it.

The wait now has an end.  Two seconds is long enough for a resource
that is going to come and short enough that one that is not going to
come does not hold the boot for ever.

The resource is named on the panel before the wait and after it, so that
a picture says which one it was:

  nio: WAIT IOBSD
  nio: WAITED

--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
@@ -5463,7 +5463,33 @@
 		LOG("config(%p): stalling %s\n", IOSERVICE_OBFUSCATE(IOThreadSelf()), getName());
 	}
 
-	waitForService( table );
+	/* nio: the wait is bounded.
+	 *
+	 * This is where the boot stopped: the platform expert's start() waits
+	 * for the resources its nub asks for, and the one it asks for is the
+	 * BSD resource, which the storage driver publishes.  This port's storage
+	 * driver matches for observation only and never publishes it, so the
+	 * wait had no end: the panel showed the mark that says a personality is
+	 * being started, and the mark that says it came back never came.
+	 *
+	 * The wait now has an end.  Two seconds is long enough for a resource
+	 * that is going to come, and short enough that one that is not going
+	 * to come does not hold the boot for ever.  The userland does not need
+	 * the BSD resource: the root volume the kernel boots from is the
+	 * mockfs image in RAM, and mockfs is the file system that mounts it.
+	 *
+	 * The resource is named on the panel before the wait and after it, so
+	 * that a picture says which one it was. */
+	nio_u_mark("\nnio: WAIT ");
+	nio_u_mark(str ? str->getCStringNoCopy() : "?");
+	{
+		mach_timespec_t nio_to;
+	
+		nio_to.tv_sec = 2;
+		nio_to.tv_nsec = 0;
+		waitForService( table, &nio_to );
+	}
+	nio_u_mark("\nnio: WAITED");
 
